ength of the semi-major axis of the ellipse." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503755 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f2c3caea-6032-5d20-9fea-52c78472efb1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503755 #edb/ref 1074 "The matrix $A$ maps the unit circle $\\{x\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ from ${R}^{2}$ onto the ellipse $\\{Ax\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}$ in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nFirst, let's re-write the squared distance from the origin to the point $Ax$ on our ellipse, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Ax{‖}^{2} & =(Ax)^{T}(Ax) \\\\  & =({x}^{T}\\,{A}^{T})(Ax) \\\\  & ={x}^{T}\\,({A}^{T}\\,A)x,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nwhere ${x}^{T}({A}^{T}A)x$ is the quadratic form with the symmetric matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe maximum value of this quadratic form, when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of the matrix ${A}^{T}A.$ \n\nThe square roots of the eigenvalues of ${A}^{T}A$ are called the *singular values* of $A.$ Since the largest singular value of $A$ is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8,$ the largest eigenvalue of ${A}^{T}A$ must be ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\sigma }_{1}^{2}={8}^{2}.$ So, we obtain that\n\n$$\n{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax{‖}^{2}\\}={8}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{max}_{‖x‖=1}\\{‖Ax‖\\}=8.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the semi-major axis of our ellipse is ${\\sigma }_{1}=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503778 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503778 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "410ad0cd-66aa-589e-9d24-b7c90f23a34c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503778 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186269243 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503786 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503786 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "52f281c4-dfee-54b8-b75f-07ea8b7aff89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503786 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186162363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503797 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "74ddedfd-6201-5f03-9796-484a0235a391" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503797 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186388618 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186503801 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186503778 #edb/ref